course: dictionary
lesson: 10145
title: Throaty: a deep, rough sound
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A throaty sound is low and rough, as if it starts deep down in your throat. «Her throaty laugh filled the whole room.» You can tell from the words around it that her laugh is deep and rough, not high and bright.
explain: The word can also describe machines. «The throaty roar of the motorbike made everyone turn around.» An engine that sounds deep and rumbly can be called throaty. Ask yourself: is the sound low and rough? If it is, throaty fits.
explain: Look at the word parts: throat plus y. Your throat is the part at the back of your mouth, where your voice comes from. So throaty means "like it comes from the throat." When you know the parts, a new word becomes easier to work out.
fact: Throaty means low and rough, as if it comes from deep in the throat.
fact: Throatier and throatiest compare sounds: a throatier voice is rougher than a throaty one.
fact: A voice can sound throaty when someone has a cold or is very emotional.
fact: «Middle English is English from about 1100 to 1500; in it the k in knight was said, and gh was a throaty sound like the ch in Scottish "loch".»
fact: A throaty sound is the opposite of a high, thin, squeaky sound.
exercise: «Her throaty laugh filled the whole room.» What kind of laugh is this? | A quiet, whispered giggle | A high, thin, squeaky laugh | A loud, bright shout | *A low, rough laugh that seems to come from deep down
exercise: Ben says the motorbike sounds throaty. What does he mean? | It is painted in bright colours | It sounds high and whistling | It has no engine at all | *It has a deep, rumbling roar
exercise: «The old man spoke in a throaty voice.» Find and copy the word that means low and rough. | *throaty | old | voice | spoke
test: When Mum has a bad cold, her voice sounds throaty. What does that mean? | She is singing a song | Her voice is high and squeaky | *Her voice is low and rough | Her voice is too quiet to hear
test: Which word best matches throaty? | Shrill | Silent | Bright | *Gruff
exam: Which sentence uses throaty correctly? | The bell rang with a sharp, tinkling sound | *The old dog gave a throaty growl from deep in its chest | She wrote her name in throaty letters | The sunny day was throaty and warm
